[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified end-to-end reachability across all user “groups” (VLANs/subnets) in the lab and found no routing or inter-VLAN issues. From the putative reporter’s segment (VLAN 5, e.g., pc1 10.5.0.1), I can reach every other group: VLAN 6 (pc3/pc4), VLAN 10 (pc5–pc8), VLAN 20 (pc9–pc11), and VLAN 30 (pc12–pc14), all with 0% loss. Bidirectional checks also succeed: for example, VLAN 20 (pc10) reaches VLAN 5 (pc1) and VLAN 30 (pc12); VLAN 30 (pc12) reaches VLAN 5 (pc1); and VLAN 5 reaches VLAN 30 (pc12/pc13/pc14). The topology-wide reachability sweep returned clean results between representative hosts in each VLAN, indicating the L3 gatewaying and routing tables are behaving correctly.

Mechanism: If a routing/route-list fault existed in the routers, we would see consistent cross-VLAN failures for that entire subnet from multiple sources, but we observe universal success across VLANs instead. Localization: No specific device or interface shows loss; return TTLs and latencies are consistent with one-hop inter-VLAN routing.

Root cause: No network-side fault found; the issue is not reproducible in the lab and is likely host-specific (e.g., local firewall, stale ARP/route cache, or DNS/application-layer resolution) on the reporter’s PC rather than a router route-list problem.

Corrective fix: On the affected PC, clear ARP/route caches, verify default gateway and subnet mask, disable/adjust host firewall temporarily, and test by IP. If issue persists, capture on the PC to identify local blocking; no changes are required on network devices. 
=== END ===